.TH LPM 1 "2026" "lpm v0.9.2" "Ludis Package Manager"
.SH NAME
lpm \- package manager for Wine/Proton runners and a Lutris game library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lpm
.RI [ command ] " [options] [arguments...]"
.br
.B lpm
.I file.zgp
| \fIfile.zgr\fR
.br
.B lpm
(no argument: graphical menu)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lpm
packages, installs, uninstalls and isolates Windows games running under Wine via
Lutris, along with their associated Wine/Proton runners, as
.I .zgp
(games) and
.I .zgr
(runners) archives.
.PP
It is a strictly
.B personal
package manager: a game already installed under a given slug can never be overwritten by
reinstalling the same slug (see
.B install
below), and no integrity check is built into the
.IR .zgp / .zgr
packages themselves \(em these packages are produced and consumed locally, not distributed
publicly.
.PP
Launched with no argument,
.B lpm
opens a graphical menu (Zenity). Launched with a single argument that is a
.I .zgp
or
.IR .zgr " file (double-click from a file manager), it installs it directly."
.SH COMMANDS \(em GAMES
.TP
.BI "install [-y] " files.zgp...
Installs one or more games from
.I .zgp
archives. Flatly refuses if the game's slug is already installed (no update path:
uninstall then reinstall if needed).
.TP
.BI "uninstall [-y] " slugs...
Uninstalls one or more installed games (Wine prefix, Lutris entry, shortcuts).
.TP
.BI "pack [-level] " slugs...
Packages one or more installed games as
.I .zgp
archives.
.TP
.BI "isolate [" store ]
Fully isolates a store living in a shared giant prefix (Epic Games Store, EA App/EA
Desktop, Ubisoft Connect, Battle.net): every game currently in that giant prefix
gets its own independent Wine prefix in a single pass (never just one game isolated
while leaving the others behind).
.I store
can be an internal code
.RI ( egs ", " ea ", " ubisoft ", " battlenet ),
a common alias (e.g.
.IR epic ", " blizzard ),
or the slug of a game currently detected in that giant prefix (only used to resolve the
target store -- all of its games are isolated, not just the one named). With no argument:
interactive Zenity selection of the store to isolate among those detected (see also
.B list-isolable
below for the non-interactive version of this list, game by game with its associated store).
.TP
.B list
Lists installed games (slug then name), one per line.
.TP
.B list-isolable
Lists isolable games, i.e. currently living in a shared store giant prefix: slug, name, and
the store to target for
.BR isolate .
Non-interactive equivalent of the list offered by
.B isolate
with no argument.
.TP
.BI "info " slug
Shows the known metadata for an installed game: name, slug, Wine prefix directory,
executable, the Wine/Proton runner version used by that game, install date, and
isolation status (dedicated prefix, or shared prefix with the targeted store where
applicable, see
.BR list-isolable ).
.TP
.BI "create-prefix [-y] " names...
Creates one or more blank wineprefixes, registered directly in Lutris, without going
through an installer wizard (no script, no executable run). Reproduces Lutris's
internal initialization mechanism (wineboot for a classic Wine runner,
.I "umu-run createprefix"
for a Proton one). Each
.I name
may be followed by a custom slug, separated by a vertical bar
.RI ( "My Game|my-slug" ).
With no argument: interactive selection of the runner and architecture (32/64-bit) via
Zenity; in CLI mode, the default runner and 64-bit architecture are used.
.TP
.BI "exe-install [-y] " "file[|name[|slug]]"
Creates a blank wineprefix then launches a Windows installer inside it
.RI ( .exe / .msi / .bat / .cmd )
in the foreground, exactly like Lutris's "Install a Windows executable" wizard.
One file at a time only. The display name and slug may be supplied after the path,
separated by a vertical bar; otherwise the name is derived from the file name. After
the installer exits normally, offers to select the installed game's final executable
(file picker inside the prefix).
.TP
.BI "shortcut " "slugs...|--all"
(Re)generates the shortcuts (application menu and/or desktop) for one or more already
installed games, or for all installed Wine games with
.BR --all .
Useful after changing a game's icon (see
.B icon
below), or for a game added to Lutris by some other means than
.BR lpm .
Only writes
.I .desktop
files: no confirmation required.
.TP
.BI "icon [" "slugs...|--all" ]
Automatically fetches an icon for one or more already installed games, via
SteamGridDB (community icons and the official "Steam Client Icon" when it exists),
then regenerates their existing shortcuts to use it. Only games available on Steam
are covered (no other storefront is currently supported). Requires a personal
SteamGridDB API key, requested and saved on first use
.RI ( "${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/lpm/steamgriddb.key" ,
permissions restricted to the owner). With no argument: interactive selection among all
installed games, pre-checking those that don't yet have a custom icon.
.TP
.BI "lsfg [" "slugs..." "] [" "on" " | " "off" ]
Enables or disables lsfg-vk ("Lossless Scaling" frame generation) for one or more
installed Wine/Proton games. Installs lsfg-vk automatically if missing (Flatpak extension
.I org.freedesktop.Platform.VulkanLayer.lsfgvk
with no password prompt, or a link to the official docs on a native package). Asks,
on first activation, for the
.B lsfg-vk.dll
file from the Steam beta branch
.I lsfg-vk
of the "Lossless Scaling" game (Properties > Betas) \(em a file with any other name
(notably
.IR Lossless.dll ,
from the normal public branch) is rejected: it does not contain the shader required by
the lsfg-vk 2.0 scheme. On activation, copies this file into the game's prefix and adds
.IR LSFGVK_ENV=1 ", " LSFGVK_DLL_PATH
and
.I LSFGVK_MULTIPLIER=2
(only if absent) to its Lutris
.I system.env
; on deactivation, removes these 3 keys without ever deleting the DLL file already
copied. With no argument: interactive Zenity selection, listing only the relevant
Wine/Proton games (able to be enabled or disabled depending on their current state).
.TP
.BI "tools [" slug " [" tool " [" exe ]]]
Opens a Wine tools menu for an already installed game
.RI ( winetricks ", registry editor, " winecfg ", DOS console, run an ."
.IR exe ", open the prefix folder)."
.I tool
can be
.BR winetricks ", " regedit ", " winecfg ", " console ", " exe " or " folder .
Fully command-line (no Zenity dialog) only when both
.I slug
and
.I tool
are supplied;
.I exe
is only used for the
.B exe
tool. With no argument: interactive selection of the game then the tool.
.TP
.BI "killwine [-y]"
Immediately kills any running Wine, Winetricks, umu-run or Proton (launched by Lutris)
process, regardless of the game \(em panic button for a stuck process. Any unsaved
progress in the affected game is lost.
.SH COMMANDS \(em RUNNERS
.TP
.BI "install-runner [-y] " "files.zgr|names..."
Installs one or more Wine/Proton runners, from a local
.I .zgr
file or by name, in which case it is looked up in the reference GitHub release.
.TP
.BI "uninstall-runner [-y] " names...
Uninstalls one or more installed runners.
.TP
.BI "pack-runner [-level] " names...
Packages one or more installed runners as
.I .zgr
archives.
.TP
.B list-runner
Lists installed runners.
.TP
.B list-remote-runners
Lists runners available for download from the remote GitHub repository.
.SH DEPENDENCIES
.TP
.B check
Checks that every runner required by the installed games is actually present, and
offers to fetch any missing ones from the lpm GitHub repository.
.TP
.B lutris-version " [" flatpak " | " native " | " reset "]"
Shows the Lutris versions detected on the machine (Flatpak and/or native package),
with their version number and an up-to-date/outdated status. If both versions are
installed at the same time, lpm asks once which one to use and remembers that
choice in
.IR ~/.config/lpm/lutris-version .
With no argument, the command shows the current state; with
.B flatpak
or
.BR native ,
it forces and saves that choice; with
.BR reset ,
it clears it so it is asked again automatically on the next launch if both
versions are still present.
.SH LOG
Every run of
.BR install ", " uninstall ", " pack ", " isolate " and " icon
(success as well as failure) is logged to
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/lpm/lpm.log " (by default " ~/.local/share/lpm/lpm.log ),
one line per event, tab-separated columns: timestamp, command, status (OK/ERROR/INFO),
detail. Useful for debugging after the fact an installation or isolation that failed.
.PP
Past 10000 lines, the log is automatically rotated: the current file is renamed
.I lpm.log.1
(overwriting any previous backup) and a fresh, empty log is started.
.B log
never reads
.IR lpm.log.1 ", only the current log."
.TP
.B log
Shows the last 50 lines of the log.
.TP
.BI "log -n " N
Shows the last
.I N
lines of the log.
.TP
.B log --all
Shows the whole log.
.TP
.BI "log --grep " pattern
Filters log lines containing
.IR pattern " (command, slug, status, failure reason...)."
.TP
.B log --clear
Clears the current log and removes
.I lpm.log.1
if it exists, after interactive confirmation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-y
Confirms automatically, without asking for interactive confirmation. Applies to
.BR install ", " uninstall ", " install-runner ", " uninstall-runner ", " create-prefix ", "
.BR exe-install " and " check .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-scripts
Automatically approves the embedded launch/exit scripts
.RI ( prelaunch_command ", etc.) found in installed "
.I .zgp
packages, without asking. Applies only to
.BR install ;
reserve for trusted packages or ones you created yourself.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-hash
Skips the signature check (sha256) for
.B install
and
.BR install-runner ,
even if a sidecar hash file is present next to the archive.
.TP
.B \-\-hash
Generates a sidecar hash file (sha256) for every archive created by
.B pack
or
.BR pack-runner .
.TP
.BI \-level
zstd compression level, from 0 (fast, low compression) to 22 (slow, high compression),
for
.B pack
and
.BR pack-runner .
Given as a single glued token (e.g.
.BR \-9 ,
like gzip), not an option followed by a separate value.
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-v
Shows the lpm version.
.TP
.B \-\-help, \-h
Shows this page (or a built-in summary if the man page is not installed).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Games/<slug>/
Wine prefixes of installed games (or the custom location configured in Lutris).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lpm/lpm.log
Action log (see
.B LOG
above).
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/lpm/lpm.log.1
Backup of the previous log, created by automatic rotation (see
.B LOG
above).
.TP
.I ~/.config/lutris/ (native package) or ~/.var/app/net.lutris.Lutris/ (Flatpak)
Lutris configuration and database, read and modified by lpm throughout
installs/uninstalls/isolations.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lpm/steamgriddb.key (by default ~/.config/lpm/steamgriddb.key)
The user's personal SteamGridDB API key, used by
.BR icon .
Permissions restricted to the owner (600), never shared or distributed with lpm.
.SH SEE ALSO
The project's repository, for the full documentation (
.IR .zgp / .zgr
formats, details of per-store isolation, contributing translations).
.SH AUTHOR
Ludis Package Manager (lpm) is a personal project.
